1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:06,654
A real experiment. A language model is a program that continues text.

2
00:00:06,754 --> 00:00:10,482
We give one, GPT-2 XL, the start of a sentence, called the

3
00:00:10,582 --> 00:00:14,772
prompt: "I hate you because". Left alone, it continues like this.

4
00:00:14,866 --> 00:00:17,763
Now we run it again from the same random seed, a number

5
00:00:17,863 --> 00:00:21,305
that makes a run repeatable. This time, while the model reads the

6
00:00:21,405 --> 00:00:24,520
start of the text, we add fixed lists of numbers inside it.

7
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:27,462
Same prompt, same model. Here is the new continuation.

8
00:00:27,533 --> 00:00:30,781
A list of numbers is called a vector. A vector that changes a model's

9
00:00:30,881 --> 00:00:34,177
behavior when you add it inside the model is called a steering vector.

10
00:00:34,266 --> 00:00:37,516
These two lines are the whole idea: the first builds a steering

11
00:00:37,616 --> 00:00:40,759
vector, the second adds it. The small line below is the model

12
00:00:40,859 --> 00:00:43,736
they act on. By the end of this video, every symbol here

13
00:00:43,836 --> 00:00:46,766
will be defined from scratch, each with a worked example.

14
00:00:49,366 --> 00:00:54,269
A scalar is a single real number, like 3, minus one half, or 0.665.

15
00:00:54,369 --> 00:00:59,646
The set of all real numbers is written as a double-struck R. The rounded

16
00:00:59,746 --> 00:01:03,306
E reads "is an element of": 3 is an element of R.

17
00:01:04,399 --> 00:01:08,066
A vector is an ordered list of real numbers. Each entry is

18
00:01:08,166 --> 00:01:12,288
a component. The vector two, one has first component 2 and second

19
00:01:12,388 --> 00:01:16,251
component 1. Drawn as an arrow: two steps right, one step up.

20
00:01:16,332 --> 00:01:20,137
The number of components is the dimension, written d. All vectors

21
00:01:20,237 --> 00:01:22,961
with d real components form the set R to the d.

22
00:01:23,061 --> 00:01:25,064
So two, one is an element of R two.

23
00:01:26,132 --> 00:01:29,788
Inside GPT-2 XL, text is represented by vectors with

24
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:33,544
d equal to 1,600 components. They are computed using

25
00:01:33,644 --> 00:01:38,239
about 1.56 billion numbers, called parameters or weights, learned

26
00:01:38,339 --> 00:01:41,056
from text in a process called training.

27
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:47,644
Take a equal to one, two, and b equal to three,

28
00:01:47,744 --> 00:01:52,083
minus one. Addition adds matching components: a plus b is four,

29
00:01:52,183 --> 00:01:55,325
one. In the picture, b's tail sits at a's tip.

30
00:01:55,399 --> 00:01:59,496
Multiplying by a scalar multiplies every component. Two times

31
00:01:59,596 --> 00:02:02,592
b is six, minus two: same direction, twice as

32
00:02:02,692 --> 00:02:05,619
long. A negative scalar flips the direction.

33
00:02:05,699 --> 00:02:09,433
Subtraction adds the flipped vector: a minus b is minus two,

34
00:02:09,533 --> 00:02:12,309
three, the arrow from the tip of b to the tip

35
00:02:12,409 --> 00:02:15,823
of a. The difference of two vectors is itself a vector.

36
00:02:16,899 --> 00:02:21,182
The Greek capital sigma means: add up every term. The mean, or

37
00:02:21,282 --> 00:02:25,141
average, of n vectors is their sum times one over n. The

38
00:02:25,241 --> 00:02:28,463
mean of one, two and three, four is two, three.

39
00:02:29,533 --> 00:02:35,061
The dot product multiplies matching components and adds the results into one scalar:

40
00:02:35,161 --> 00:02:38,947
a dot b is one times three plus two times minus one, which

41
00:02:39,047 --> 00:02:43,436
is 1. The norm, written with double bars, is a vector's length: the

42
00:02:43,536 --> 00:02:47,590
square root of the vector dotted with itself. The norm of b is

43
00:02:47,690 --> 00:02:51,945
the square root of ten, about 3.16. Dividing a vector by its norm

44
00:02:52,045 --> 00:02:55,497
gives a unit vector, of length one: a pure direction.

45
00:02:58,066 --> 00:03:02,153
A function takes each input to exactly one output. f from X to

46
00:03:02,253 --> 00:03:05,598
Y takes elements of a set X to elements of a set Y.

47
00:03:06,666 --> 00:03:12,197
A language model doesn't read letters. A tokenizer, a fixed function, cuts

48
00:03:12,297 --> 00:03:17,373
text into tokens, words or pieces of words, each with a whole-number

49
00:03:17,473 --> 00:03:21,635
ID. The set of all tokens is the vocabulary, V, and bars

50
00:03:21,735 --> 00:03:26,582
around a set mean its size. GPT-2's vocabulary has 50,257 tokens.

51
00:03:27,666 --> 00:03:32,318
Our prompt becomes these tokens. Like the library the original experiment

52
00:03:32,418 --> 00:03:37,070
used, we start every text with GPT-2's special end-of-text token; GPT-2's

53
00:03:37,170 --> 00:03:40,521
own tokenizer does not add it. A token's place in the

54
00:03:40,621 --> 00:03:43,646
sequence is its position, t, counting from zero.

55
00:03:44,732 --> 00:03:48,130
An embedding is a function from a token and its position to

56
00:03:48,230 --> 00:03:51,035
a vector: it looks up a learned vector in R d for

57
00:03:51,135 --> 00:03:54,830
the token and adds another learned vector for its position. In a

58
00:03:54,930 --> 00:03:59,099
toy, three-token vocabulary, "love" might map to one, zero. Now the text

59
00:03:59,199 --> 00:04:01,648
is a sequence of vectors, one per position.

60
00:04:04,233 --> 00:04:08,065
The model then processes these vectors in a fixed number

61
00:04:08,165 --> 00:04:11,365
of steps called layers. GPT-2 XL has L equal to

62
00:04:11,465 --> 00:04:14,525
48, numbered by ell from zero to L minus one.

63
00:04:15,599 --> 00:04:19,347
Each layer has two parts, both functions. Attention lets every

64
00:04:19,447 --> 00:04:23,754
position gather information from itself and earlier positions; the MLP,

65
00:04:23,854 --> 00:04:28,099
a multilayer perceptron, transforms each position's vector on its own.

66
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:33,020
Both add their outputs onto a running vector at every position: the

67
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:36,180
residual stream. h sub ell plus one is h sub ell, plus

68
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:40,276
attention's output, plus the output of the MLP, which reads the stream

69
00:04:40,376 --> 00:04:44,372
after attention. Each part first rescales what it reads, a step called

70
00:04:44,472 --> 00:04:48,761
normalization, but its output is simply added. As Elhage and colleagues put

71
00:04:48,861 --> 00:04:52,155
it, the residual stream is simply the sum of the output of

72
00:04:52,255 --> 00:04:55,139
all the previous layers and the original embedding.

73
00:04:55,833 --> 00:05:00,277
In two dimensions: the stream starts at one, zero. Attention writes

74
00:05:00,377 --> 00:05:04,212
zero, one, giving one, one. The MLP writes one half, minus

75
00:05:04,312 --> 00:05:07,061
one half, giving one and a half, one half.

76
00:05:07,733 --> 00:05:12,547
h sub ell, superscript t, is the residual stream entering layer ell

77
00:05:12,647 --> 00:05:17,901
at position t. Numbers computed inside a model like this are activations;

78
00:05:18,001 --> 00:05:22,155
one run through all layers is a forward pass. In GPT-2 XL,

79
00:05:22,255 --> 00:05:25,969
the stream entering layer 6 at "hate" has norm 88.7.

80
00:05:28,566 --> 00:05:31,808
To turn the final vector back into text, the model uses a

81
00:05:31,908 --> 00:05:36,030
matrix, a rectangular grid of numbers. Matrix times vector takes the dot

82
00:05:36,130 --> 00:05:39,314
product of each row with the vector: one number per row.

83
00:05:40,399 --> 00:05:43,435
The unembedding matrix, W sub U, has one row per

84
00:05:43,535 --> 00:05:47,682
vocabulary token. Times the final residual stream, after one last

85
00:05:47,782 --> 00:05:51,603
normalization, it gives one score per token, called a logit.

86
00:05:52,999 --> 00:05:58,555
Softmax turns logits into probabilities: raise e to each logit,

87
00:05:58,655 --> 00:06:03,404
then divide by the total. Logits two, one, zero become

88
00:06:03,504 --> 00:06:08,163
0.665, 0.245 and 0.090: all positive, summing to one.

89
00:06:09,232 --> 00:06:12,258
The model then samples: it picks the next token at

90
00:06:12,358 --> 00:06:16,385
random with those probabilities, appends it, and repeats. Our runs

91
00:06:16,485 --> 00:06:20,574
use the original experiment's settings, which sample only among the

92
00:06:20,674 --> 00:06:24,701
most likely tokens and discourage repeats. After our prompt, GPT-2

93
00:06:24,801 --> 00:06:28,452
XL's most likely next token is "you", with probability 0.59.

94
00:06:31,033 --> 00:06:33,633
Why would adding a vector change behavior? A unit

95
00:06:33,733 --> 00:06:37,490
vector describes a direction, and the linear representation hypothesis

96
00:06:37,590 --> 00:06:40,851
is the idea that high-level concepts are represented linearly

97
00:06:40,951 --> 00:06:43,221
as directions in some representation space.

98
00:06:44,299 --> 00:06:48,218
An early clue came from word vectors: Mikolov and colleagues reported

99
00:06:48,318 --> 00:06:51,422
that King minus Man plus Woman results in a vector very

100
00:06:51,522 --> 00:06:54,975
close to Queen. Woman minus Man acts like a gender direction.

101
00:06:55,666 --> 00:06:58,619
A toy picture: if one axis meant how positive the text

102
00:06:58,719 --> 00:07:01,898
is, moving a vector along it would make later layers treat

103
00:07:01,998 --> 00:07:05,574
the text as more positive. Steering looks for such directions and

104
00:07:05,674 --> 00:07:08,627
pushes along them. But it is a hypothesis: it can hold

105
00:07:08,727 --> 00:07:11,454
for some concepts and layers, and fail for others.

106
00:07:14,033 --> 00:07:17,854
To find a direction, compare two inputs that differ mainly in

107
00:07:17,954 --> 00:07:21,519
one concept: a contrast pair, p plus and p minus. ActAdd,

108
00:07:21,619 --> 00:07:26,147
short for Activation Addition, by Turner and colleagues, uses "Love" and

109
00:07:26,247 --> 00:07:29,941
"Hate" and needs no training: a single pair of data points.

110
00:07:31,041 --> 00:07:35,865
Run each prompt through the model, read the residual stream entering layer

111
00:07:35,965 --> 00:07:39,325
ell, and subtract: h A equals h ell of p plus, minus

112
00:07:39,425 --> 00:07:43,717
h ell of p minus, one difference per position. GPT-2 splits "Hate"

113
00:07:43,817 --> 00:07:47,045
into two tokens, so "Love" is padded with a space.

114
00:07:47,741 --> 00:07:53,263
One pair is noisy. CAA, Contrastive Activation Addition, by Rimsky and colleagues, averages

115
00:07:53,363 --> 00:07:57,093
hundreds of pairs to reduce that noise. It uses a dataset D of

116
00:07:57,193 --> 00:08:01,295
triples: a prompt p, a completion c plus showing the behavior, and c

117
00:08:01,395 --> 00:08:04,693
minus showing its opposite. a sub ell of p and c is the

118
00:08:04,793 --> 00:08:08,833
residual stream at layer ell, at the position of c's answer letter.

119
00:08:09,507 --> 00:08:13,787
So: take each triple's difference of activations, add them up with sigma,

120
00:08:13,887 --> 00:08:17,267
and multiply by one over the size of D. A mean difference.

121
00:08:17,367 --> 00:08:20,507
ActAdd is like the case where D holds one pair, but it

122
00:08:20,607 --> 00:08:23,927
has no completions and keeps one difference per position.

123
00:08:25,607 --> 00:08:29,229
Toy example: differences two point one, zero point three;

124
00:08:29,329 --> 00:08:32,429
one point eight, minus zero point five; two point

125
00:08:32,529 --> 00:08:35,498
one, zero point two. Their mean is exactly two,

126
00:08:35,598 --> 00:08:40,003
zero: the shared direction survives, the unrelated component cancels.

127
00:08:40,674 --> 00:08:44,724
In GPT-2 XL at layer 6, look at position one: the "Love"

128
00:08:44,824 --> 00:08:49,245
token and "H", the first token of "Hate", have norms of about

129
00:08:49,345 --> 00:08:54,210
98.0 and 97.5, and cosine similarity, the dot product of their unit

130
00:08:54,310 --> 00:08:59,546
vectors, of 0.72. Their difference there has norm 73.8. At position zero

131
00:08:59,646 --> 00:09:05,030
both share the end-of-text token, so the difference there is exactly zero.

132
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:11,829
Steering is one addition. During the forward pass, at layer ell and

133
00:09:11,929 --> 00:09:15,641
each chosen position t, h becomes h plus alpha times v, the

134
00:09:15,741 --> 00:09:20,552
steered activation h tilde. Alpha is a scalar, the steering coefficient: its

135
00:09:20,652 --> 00:09:25,204
size sets strength, its sign direction. The weights never change; in the

136
00:09:25,304 --> 00:09:29,340
ActAdd paper's words, these vectors act like virtual bias terms.

137
00:09:30,433 --> 00:09:34,408
T is the set of edited positions. ActAdd adds row t of h A

138
00:09:34,508 --> 00:09:39,749
at each front position t of the prompt, a different vector at each position.

139
00:09:39,849 --> 00:09:44,949
CAA adds its single vector at all token positions after the user's prompt.

140
00:09:45,633 --> 00:09:50,353
Toy example, treating h as the final stream: h is one, one; v is

141
00:09:50,453 --> 00:09:54,872
two, zero; alpha is one and a half; so h tilde is four, one.

142
00:09:54,972 --> 00:10:00,972
With unembedding rows one, zero for "love", minus one, zero for "hate", and zero,

143
00:10:01,072 --> 00:10:06,094
one for "the", the logits go from one, minus one, one to four, minus

144
00:10:06,194 --> 00:10:10,613
four, one, and after softmax "love" rises from 0.47 to 0.95.

145
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:14,590
Now GPT-2 XL with the original settings: "Love" minus

146
00:10:14,690 --> 00:10:18,940
"Hate", before layer 6, coefficient plus 5. Next-token probabilities

147
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:22,970
shift; "they" gains most. Then the first sampled continuations,

148
00:10:23,070 --> 00:10:26,745
from seed zero, not cherry-picked; the original blog post's

149
00:10:26,845 --> 00:10:29,176
